# <%.Name%>

A [Concord](https://github.com/JMThomas00/Concord) plugin that's also a
standalone program: the same binary runs in your terminal, or on a Concord
server<% if eq .Kind "bot" %> as a bot in its channels<% else %> in its own channels<% end %>.

## Run it on your own computer

<% if eq .Kind "pty" %>This plugin shows a terminal program inside Concord channels, so it only
does something useful on a Concord server (Linux or macOS). Run standalone,
it just says so.
<% else %>**Download:** from this repo's [Releases](https://github.com/<%.Repo%>/releases),
get the zip for your system (`<%.ID%>_windows_amd64.zip`,
`<%.ID%>_darwin_arm64.zip` for Apple silicon, `<%.ID%>_linux_amd64.zip`, ...),
unzip it, and run the program inside from a terminal:

```sh
./<%.ID%>          # Windows: .\<%.ID%>.exe
```

On macOS, if it's blocked as being from an unidentified developer, run
`xattr -d com.apple.quarantine <%.ID%>` once.

**Or with Go installed:**

```sh
go install <%.Module%>@latest
<%.ID%>
```
<% if eq .Kind "game" %>
It starts with a menu: two players on one keyboard, against the computer, or
over the network (one player hosts and is shown an address and a join code;
the other joins with them).
<% end %><% end %>
## Use it on a Concord server

You need to be the server owner, or have the **Manage Plugins** permission.

1. In Concord, open **Server Settings → Plugins** and press **I** (install).
2. Type `<%.Repo%>` and press Enter. Concord downloads the latest release for
   the server's own system, verifies it, and starts it: no restart, no files
   to edit.
3. Optional: select the plugin and press **Enter** for its settings.
4. Open **Server Settings → Channels**, create a channel, and choose
   **<% if eq .Kind "game" %><%.Name%> Table<% else %><%.Name%><% end %>** as its type. Its options appear there too.
<% if eq .Kind "bot" %>5. Chat in that channel (or @mention the bot anywhere).
<% else %>5. Select the channel, then press **Tab** (or click the pane) so your keys go
   to it. **Esc** (or **Ctrl+]**) gives the keyboard back to Concord; **Tab**
   moves on to the member list.<% if eq .Kind "game" %> **M** opens the table menu.<% end %>
<% end %>
To update later: select it in **Server Settings → Plugins**, press **U**, then
Enter. A failed update rolls back by itself.

## Develop

```sh
go run .              # run it standalone
go test ./...         # tests, including a fake Concord server (sdk/plugintest)
go run release.go     # the release zips, in dist/
```

Release a version by pushing a tag: `git tag v0.1.0 && git push --tags`.
`.github/workflows/release.yml` then attaches
`dist/<%.ID%>_<os>_<arch>.zip` for every platform to the GitHub release,
which is what Concord installs.

- `plugin.toml`: what Concord reads: the channel type this plugin adds, its
  settings (shown as forms in Concord; never hand-edited), and which binary
  to start on each OS.
- `main.go`: standalone vs plugin, and the plugin's behaviour, built on the
  Concord SDK (`github.com/JMThomas00/Concord/sdk`).
